# Set up Loupe

These steps set up Loupe at {{ instance }} for the current repository. Do the steps in order. Stop and ask the user when a step needs them. Never invent or write a token, a password or a URL that the user did not give you.

## 1. Pick your section

Steps 6 and 7 are different for each agent. Read the section for the agent you run in: Claude Code or Codex. If you are not sure which agent you are, ask the user.

## 2. Check the operating system

The `loupe` CLI runs on macOS and Linux, on amd64 and arm64. It has no Windows build. If this machine runs Windows, stop and tell the user this.

## 3. Install the CLI

Run `loupe version`. If it prints a version, go to step 4.

If it fails, install the latest CLI release. A CLI release has a tag of the form `cli/vX.Y.Z`. Find the version, the platform and the archive:

```bash
version=$(curl -fsSL "https://api.github.com/repos/ubermuda/loupe/releases?per_page=100" | grep -o '"tag_name": *"cli/v[^"]*"' | head -n 1 | sed 's/.*cli\/v//; s/"$//')
os=$(uname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
arch=$(uname -m)
case "$arch" in x86_64) arch=amd64 ;; aarch64) arch=arm64 ;; esac
archive="loupe_${version}_${os}_${arch}.tar.gz"
echo "$archive"
```

The archive name must look like `loupe_1.4.0_darwin_arm64.tar.gz`. If the version is empty, or the platform is not `darwin` or `linux` with `amd64` or `arm64`, stop and show the output to the user.

Download the archive and `checksums.txt` into an empty directory, and check the archive:

```bash
cd "$(mktemp -d)"
curl -fsSLO "https://github.com/ubermuda/loupe/releases/download/cli/v${version}/${archive}"
curl -fsSLO "https://github.com/ubermuda/loupe/releases/download/cli/v${version}/checksums.txt"
shasum -a 256 -c --ignore-missing checksums.txt
```

On Linux without `shasum`, run `sha256sum -c --ignore-missing checksums.txt`. The command must print `OK` after the archive name. If it prints `FAILED`, stop, tell the user, and do not install the archive.

Put the binary in `~/.local/bin`:

```bash
tar -xzf "$archive" loupe
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin
mv loupe ~/.local/bin/
```

The CLI updates itself, so keep it in a directory the user can write. If `~/.local/bin` is not on the `PATH`, tell the user how to add it. Until then, use `~/.local/bin/loupe` in the next steps. Run `loupe version` again. It must print a version.

## 4. Sign in

The CLI keeps one sign-in, in `config.json` in its configuration directory: `~/Library/Application Support/loupe/` on macOS, `~/.config/loupe/` on Linux. If that file has `"baseUrl": "{{ instance }}"`, go to step 5. If a later step says `not logged in`, come back to this step.

`loupe login` waits until the user approves the sign-in in a browser. Start it in the background, with its output in a log file. `nohup` keeps it alive when your shell call ends:

```bash
nohup loupe login --url {{ instance }} > /tmp/loupe-login.log 2>&1 &
```

Read `/tmp/loupe-login.log` until it shows a page URL and a code. Show both to the user, and ask them to open the page, check the code and choose Allow. Then read the log again until the command ends. It ends with `Logged in. Tokens saved.` If it ends with an error, show the error to the user and stop.

## 5. Bind the repository

Run the commands in this step and the next steps from the root of the repository.

{% if projectId -%}
The project id is `{{ id }}`.

If `.loupe.yaml` exists, read it. When its `project` is `{{ id }}`, the file is correct. In Codex, go to step 7. In Claude Code, still run the command below, because it keeps the file and declares the server. When its `project` is another id, ask the user before you replace it. Add `--force` to the command below only when the user agrees.

In Claude Code, run:

```bash
loupe init --project {{ id }} --mcp < /dev/null
```

In Codex, run:

```bash
loupe init --project {{ id }} --no-mcp < /dev/null
```
{%- else -%}
Nobody gave a project id, so find it. If `.loupe.yaml` exists, it already names the project. Use its `project` value as the project id, and do not run the command below.

Run:

```bash
loupe init --no-mcp < /dev/null
```

The output tells you what to do next:

- `Your login covers one project` means the command wrote `.loupe.yaml`. Use its `project` value as the project id.
- `Which project does this repository belong to?` comes before a list of projects, each with its id. Show the list to the user and ask which project to use. Then run `loupe init --project <project id> --no-mcp < /dev/null`.
- `your login covers no project` means the user has no project yet. Ask the user to create a project at {{ instance }} in a browser, and stop.

In Claude Code, then run:

```bash
loupe init --mcp < /dev/null
```
{%- endif %}


In Claude Code, `--mcp` declares the `loupe` MCP server at user scope. Never use `--mcp-json`. If the output contains `Left it.`, another server already has the name `loupe`. Show the output to the user and ask whether to run the command it names. When the user agrees, run that command, then run the `loupe init` command for Claude Code again.

## 6. Claude Code only

Step 5 declared the MCP server. Install the Loupe skills:

```bash
claude plugin marketplace add ubermuda/loupe
claude plugin install loupe@loupe
```

Go to step 8.

## 7. Codex only

Declare the MCP server with the project id:

```bash
codex mcp add loupe -- loupe mcp --project {{ id }}
```

If Codex already has a server named `loupe`, show it to the user and ask before you replace it.

Copy the Loupe skills into `~/.agents/skills/`:

```bash
source=$(mktemp -d)
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/ubermuda/loupe.git "$source/loupe"
mkdir -p ~/.agents/skills
for skill in "$source"/loupe/plugins/loupe/skills/*/; do
  name=$(basename "$skill")
  if [ -e ~/.agents/skills/"$name" ]; then echo "exists: $name"; else cp -R "${skill%/}" ~/.agents/skills/"$name"; fi
done
```

The loop does not replace a skill that exists. For each `exists:` line, ask the user before you replace that skill.

## 8. Check

Run `loupe status`. It asks Loupe for the current project and prints its name.

If the command is unknown, run `loupe update`, then run `loupe status` again. If it is still unknown, skip this step.

If `loupe status` fails, show its output to the user and stop.

## 9. Finish

Tell the user to restart the agent, because an agent reads its MCP servers when it starts. In the new session, call the `project_current` tool and tell the user the project name it gives.
